{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for N1's exact design/source delivery at 75260d65112689f67e9d78b2e76094f0aa7dc7bf, invitation ad2c9cd3 and my promise 38e582cd. Primary 66c9f481 first; all four subjects reviewed. The bounded-read contract needs two small corrections before adoption. The original request 53016b8e owes these design/specification source notes and implementing-lane scopes. Normal assigned prepare binds only to that request and promise 4ba647c2. No runtime routes, C5/C6 delivery, successor implementation or adopted wire behavior are established by this review.\n\nP2 — Bound repository commit and tree inputs before allocation. Design lines 244–247, 258–261 and 479–520 bound counts and output fields: 4,000 commit reads, 16 returned parents, 256-byte subjects/change identities, 100,000 examined entries and a 2 MiB answer. The explicit pre-read byte guarantee at 311–316 covers blobs only. A commit message or one tree entry's name can be arbitrarily large under the stated design while counting as one object. Trimming a returned subject or refusing a long returned path after reading and parsing that object does not bound the input allocation. The historical TreeReader returns parsed objects/whole arrays; treeDiff at d691e6e9 reads a whole tree at lines 135–146 before counting entries at 186–189. That source supports the missing design condition, not a claim that the actual provider has an unbounded response.\n\nRequire lane A2 to establish a finite input-byte and decoded-allocation budget for commit and tree reads, including messages, headers, parent lists, entry names and arrays. Bounded host projection, verified preflight size, or capped streaming may satisfy it. If the host cannot supply a safe bounded read, return an explicit qualified refusal and preserve unavailable versus absent semantics. Define the relevant A1 error/value grammar and A3 behavior; do not return an incomplete parent set, tree or history as complete. Exact numerical limits and host capability evidence remain owned implementation decisions, rather than a new test quota. The head-file path should avoid unnecessary history work while still applying these object-input protections. A compact oversized-input refusal example is sufficient design evidence now.\n\nP2 — Represent the total size of a streamed symlink honestly. Lines 311–316 permit an adapter that has streaming but no size metadata and stops one byte past the bound. Yet the link row at 301 and ProposalFile at 654 require an exact bytes number even when the target is omitted. If an oversized symlink target is stopped at its limit, the adapter knows a lower bound but not the total length. It cannot satisfy both rules by inventing a total or downloading the remainder.\n\nMake link bytes optional when the total is unknown, with an explicit target-omission reason, or specify an existing qualified bounded-file outcome for that case. Align the table, union, R-ART-6, adapter handoff and acceptance grammar. Never report the observed prefix length as total size. Keep targets inert and never followed. This is a permitted adapter-case contradiction in the unimplemented design; it is not an observed provider failure and needs no live probe or new test sweep.\n\nSmall corrections alongside those repairs. Lines 523–525 call the 2,000-commit set limit an existing value. Historical DiffBounds.maxCommits bounds merge-base reads; the newly defined generation set has its own proposed limit. Correct that provenance wording. Specify lossless valid UTF-8 text decoding, preserving an initial BOM, CR/LF and final-newline distinctions. Existing artifacts.text uses TextDecoder with ignoreBOM:false for configuration text; that would strip a BOM if copied. No new file decoder exists, so this is implementation guidance rather than a demonstrated N1 implementation defect.\n\nArchitecture. Serving exact immutable inputs and putting presentation in one shared public client module is a coherent division of work. The design explicitly records empty versus complete merge-base comparisons for future proposal generations. It retains all bases in a criss-cross case, distinguishes a legacy candidate from a recorded fact, and avoids consulting present main to infer a historical comparison. The history set is separated from work needed to find it, has a deterministic parent-before-child order and explicitly includes boundary parents for complete first-parent patches. The head and recorded bases remain available without listing the history. commitChanges resolves the otherwise missing add-then-remove path within a generation. Root and merge semantics and the difference between a proposal's net change and a commit's own patch are stated.\n\nThe declared history/entry/page/response limits and qualified incomplete outcomes are useful. Response-budget paging progresses or explicitly refuses an unreturnable path; it never renames a path by truncation. No partial history list is passed off as the complete set. The client must obtain all commit-change pages before calling a patch complete, identify unsupported comparisons and binary/large/unbounded content, and never treat a clean comparable subset as ready to review. Those conditions should survive the input-byte repair. This does not prove successor capacity or current public route availability.\n\nSecurity. Whole-tree read access is an explicit proposed disclosure to every currently authenticated active member and valid delegate, including files unchanged by the proposal and committed secrets. It is neither silently inherited from metadata access nor limited by an unimplemented policy. Qualified independent reviewers receive a path without a fork write credential. Uncommitted workspace, transcript and command access remains separately owned. Reads resolve a permitted snapshot by path; naming an arbitrary blob or a commit merely on present main does not grant access. Symbols and submodule identifiers are inert data. Render file text, headers, paths and target strings inertly; no HTML interpretation, loads, execution or link following.\n\nAuthority must be checked at the start and immediately before content is returned, including cached reads. New pages repeat authorization. A cache cannot preserve permission or latest-generation state. Revocation stops later reads but cannot recall already received bytes. HTTPS no-store is a useful transport instruction, not a promise of remote erasure. Reader decisions and narrower policy-based disclosure remain explicit adoption work. Current successor authority/freshness belongs to R3; this review does not authorize a cached credential to substitute for it.\n\nFailure grammar and reviewer value. Unknown lane/generation, wrong head, forbidden snapshot, malformed query and unavailable content remain distinct. A missing expected object or host error must never become deleted, absent, empty diff or unknown comparison. Directory-as-absent is explicitly file-read semantics, with no directory-listing claim. Reads of an earlier generation keep its exact subject and report latest only as advisory. A reviewer can ask a line-bound question, deliberately handle a new generation, distinguish here/carried/policy-rejected/stale evidence and compare the proposal sequence with the published watermark. A rendered diff or author-supplied change identity informs judgment without deciding obligations or carrying. Duplicate or missing author identities remain uncertain. The full cold-reviewer path and the failing/incomplete path are useful required outcomes, not demonstrated usability evidence.\n\nSimplification. Keep one immutable read surface, one snapshot adapter and one shared diff/line-mapping module used by browser and MCP. Preserve exact identity through slices and report completeness explicitly. Do not introduce a hidden Room hunk engine, raw-object public escape hatch, private transcript dependency or app-specific signing path. The two repairs belong in that same contract and adapter scope. Implementation should reuse compact existing conformance evidence at the cheapest relevant boundary, with selected external capability evidence where local adapters cannot prove fidelity. No numerical test quota or universal Jam gate follows from this review.\n\nFull scope and current retarget direction. I read the entire saved 006 map, its commissioning ledger and separate provenance. All ten scenarios, N1–N7, full C1–C6 browser journeys, public-package outsider starter, complete 67-page inventory plus additional manual material, absent attention and retained duty settlement remain owed. The copied map and ledger preserve their exact recorded reviewed byte hashes; the separate provenance accurately describes their annotations as attributed ledger claims rather than a new builder adoption. I have not reratified those historical approval facts merely by reading the files.\n\nHugh's later d4e07064 direction and the current jointly reviewed/adopted 017/018 scope govern successor retargeting. N1's Room/generation vocabulary and legacy field strategy are predecessor design context, not a new backward-compatibility obligation or permission to reintroduce an old engine/reader. Before successor implementation, the owner must map these useful exact-artifact, bound, disclosure and failure requirements into the independently adopted R1/R2/R3/R4 reference, authority and retention contracts, and give every retained or removed component its actual task/disposition. No adapter bridge is demanded by this review. Accepted requests and real external duties still need their proper attributed reconciliation; a model replacement does not settle them.\n\nThe seven implementing lanes remain concrete design handoffs: A1 contract; A2 Git adapter and consumer-first record support; A3 Room/read authority/cache/load/transport; A4 shared public client and snapshot/diff module; A5 live screens; A6 cold-reviewer acceptance; A7 bounded MCP presentation with explicit file/diff identities and completeness. Main-file MCP access stays its separately owned follow-up. A4 cannot use the existing object-ID CommitStore code unchanged, which the design correctly acknowledges. Actual host bounded-read capability, work throttle/cache sizing and diff computation budgets require owned implementation evidence. A design report is not their delivery.\n\nIndependent evidence. I read every byte of all four exact subjects: the main design 55,696 bytes/999 lines, SHA256 10deccd0e84175656829ab5cad6bd2a0bfb459b4f629fdafd360adee75027598; saved map 24,013 bytes/321 lines, SHA256 7bdca0fd19a8c26613c9e9ea427c6cea1c3296c7fa429ff3ff1561135e03a381; ledger 9,143 bytes/116 lines, SHA256 763afb79e879ae59da2f0096108b894ab3a168e31ec274ab09af14dc0cb28fdd; provenance 2,270 bytes/52 lines, SHA256 629a2427cda0e83fb2292524e082a45cb0c6ae40aa33eab77527c0456d472fae. Each raw artifact payload head/path matches the exact Git blob, and each sole raw Rests-On is original promise4ba647c2. The registered isolated review checkout is clean at the exact head. I read the full original N1 conditions and invitation. Historical source spot checks were at the stated d691e6e9: full artifacts adapter, tree diff, existing change-history module and read module, plus authentication and HTTP/live adapter excerpts. They qualify the historical claims, not present-day delivery or exhaustive source correctness.\n\nOne user-authorized parallel static audit assisted the object-bound and streamed-link checks. I independently read all four subjects and verified both triggers and remedies against the contract. The provider's actual projection/streaming behavior was not verified. No source changes, tests, install, typecheck, build, repository-code execution, private diagnostic, live-provider effect, deployment or person contact occurred. The earlier rejected private diagnostic was not rerun, rewritten, extended or delegated; no native reviewer/task/model/backend replacement or restriction override was used.\n\nBinding correction. My accepted promise initially called this evidence-only. The normal guard correctly refused that option: original N1 owes assigned Git design-source artifacts. I recorded the correction in 9b024b98 and use the ordinary assigned design-delivery binding, all four subjects and original request53016b8e. No binding override or disguised verdict is used. Changes requested authorize no landing or runtime implementation. Correct the two bounded-adapter/grammar conditions, preserve the complete commissioning, publish exact revised source evidence, and return for normal independent review before design adoption and any separately commissioned implementation.\n\nMandatory head news. I read the complete records 6f2ddd2e and 9b024b98 required by the normal guard and acknowledge both. The first is planner publication/delta and scheduling context, expressly no independent approval, adoption or implementation commission; the second is my explicit binding correction. Neither changes the inspected exact subjects or cures the two findings. The guard includes my own accepted promise as its acknowledgment automatically.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:66c9f4811c4beac0758b0a8c288a87ed8dc6e650","binding":"assigned","head":"75260d65112689f67e9d78b2e76094f0aa7dc7bf","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6f2ddd2e28a864f2dbe4ba8d4e9d64d5b9b4e2ca\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:38e582cda12589e930b46f706cf9c15a71ff0bda\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9b024b982b06831fcc22c0a6530f396d46e8fa02\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4ba647c2e18d9cfcbce10f1d08cfcbcf764aecd2\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9b024b982b06831fcc22c0a6530f396d46e8fa02","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}